  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Up to now we have been considering the identity of single genes by descent. Another useful measure is the probability that single-locus genotypes (both genes) of two individuals are identical by descent. The formulation of such a measure, which we denote as $ \\Delta_{xy} $, is attributable to Cotterman (1954) and was called the coefficient of fraternity by Trustrum (1961). The problem is set out in Figure 7.5. Here we denote the mothers of individuals x and y as $ m_x $ and $ m_y $, and the fathers as $ f_x $ and $ f_y $. The coefficients of coancestry $ \\Theta_{m_x m_y} $, $ \\Theta_{m_x f_y} $, $ \\Theta_{f_x m_y} $, and $ \\Theta_{f_x f_y} $ provide measures of the probability of drawing genes identical by descent from all four combinations of parents.",
      "source_page": 156,
      "printed_page": 136,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter7:p010:b4",
        "Genetics_chapter7:p010:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        135.0,
        811.0,
        1093.0,
        1099.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        156
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
